'' =================================================================================================
''
''   File....... SD_null_predecessor.spin2
''   Purpose.... A binary that resets the P2 and touches NOTHING on the SD bus
''   Author..... Stephen M Moraco
''               -- see below for terms of use
''   E-mail..... stephen@ironsheep.biz
''   Started.... AUG 2026
''   Updated.... 19 AUG 2026
''
'' =================================================================================================
''
'' WHAT THIS IS FOR
''
'' Round 12a turned the Edge-socket wedge (#3240) into a switch: the first binary after a
'' power cycle runs clean, any binary run afterwards without a power cycle wedges. The
'' obvious reading is "a prior driver session leaves state on the card". But the wedge
'' probe runs eight mount/operate/unmount cycles inside ONE power-on and stays clean every
'' time -- so cycles 2 through 8 are all "after a prior driver session" and none of them
'' wedge. A prior session is therefore not, by itself, the trigger.
''
'' What a wedging run has that an in-binary cycle does not is the RESET WINDOW: roughly a
'' second in which the P2 drives nothing, the card sits with CS floating and unclocked, and
'' a new binary is shifted in over serial.
''
'' This program isolates that. It creates the reset and the download window and then does
'' NOTHING to the SD bus -- no driver object, no pin writes, no clocking. Run it as the
'' predecessor and then run the reproducer:
''
''   wedges  -> no driver session is required. The trigger is the reset itself, and the
''              hypothesis moves off the card's filesystem state and onto the pins
''   clean   -> a driver session genuinely is required, and the next question is which
''              part of it
''
'' THE SIZE CONFOUND, AND WHY THERE IS A PAD
''
'' Download time scales with binary size, and download time IS the float window under
'' investigation. A 5 KB predecessor would hold the pins floating for a fraction of the
'' time a 47 KB one does, so a clean result from a tiny binary would be ambiguous: it
'' could mean "no driver session, no wedge" or merely "not long enough". The DAT pad below
'' brings this binary into the same size class as SD_RT_mount_tests (~47 KB), so the two
'' predecessors differ in what they DO and not in how long they take to arrive.
''
'' ARMS
''
''   (none)        reset, do nothing, exit -- the 13a predecessor
''   -D HOLD_120S  additionally idle for 120 s before exiting, so a "does waiting clear
''                 it" test is timed by the P2 rather than by an operator with a watch
''
'' This program deliberately does NOT include micro_sd_fat32_fs. Adding it would defeat
'' the entire purpose -- the object's DAT initialisers and any mount call are exactly the
'' variable being eliminated.
''
'' =================================================================================================

CON

    _CLKFREQ        = 350_000_000

    HOLD_SECONDS    = 120                       ' Used only by the HOLD_120S arm

    ' Pad the binary into SD_RT_mount_tests' size class. See the size-confound note above.
    PAD_LONGS       = 10_000                    ' 40 KB

DAT

' Ballast. Never read, never written -- present only so this binary takes a
' comparable time to download. Named so its purpose survives a later reader.
downloadPad     LONG    0[PAD_LONGS]

PUB go() | idx

'' Reset the P2, touch nothing on the SD bus, and leave.

    debug(" ")
    debug("======================================================")
    debug("  SD Null Predecessor")
    debug("======================================================")
    debug("  This binary does NOT touch the SD bus: no driver object, no pin")
    debug("  writes, no clocking. It exists to create a P2 reset and download")
    debug("  window with no driver session attached.")
    debug("  SysClk: ", udec_(clkfreq / 1_000_000), " MHz")

#ifdef HOLD_120S
    debug("  ARM: HOLD_120S -- idling ", udec_(HOLD_SECONDS), " s before exit.")
    debug("  The card is powered and unclocked throughout.")
    repeat idx from 1 to HOLD_SECONDS
        waitms(1000)
        if idx // 10 == 0
            debug("    idle ", udec_(idx), " s of ", udec_(HOLD_SECONDS))
#else
    debug("  ARM: immediate exit (no hold)")
#endif

    debug(" ")
    debug("  Done. Run the reproducer next WITHOUT power-cycling.")
    debug("END_SESSION")

CON { license }

